[[:db/retract 17592186221955 :question/answer-fields 17592186220384] [:db/retract 17592186221955 :question/answer-fields 17592186220378] [:db/retract 17592186221955 :question/answer-fields 17592186220381] [:db/retract 17592186221955 :question/answer-fields 17592186220375] {:db/id 17592186221412 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-307f17c8-f71c-5dc2-a87d-04fc684fa18f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221174 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](images/82/82b6d431638e3abb1c1aebf818f3fbe9f2eb92c7232a3b83c93dc6cb4e563c91.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-307f17c8-f71c-5dc2-a87d-04fc684fa18f" :question/id #uuid "307f17c8-f71c-5dc2-a87d-04fc684fa18f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335653" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{3}(t)+y(t)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+(-x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-1=-1.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get $y=-1-1=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get $y=-(-1)-1=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-775c2121-b251-544b-b927-39affbfa3ce5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-775c2121-b251-544b-b927-39affbfa3ce5" :answer-field/id #uuid "775c2121-b251-544b-b927-39affbfa3ce5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c688ede3-de50-5785-8ead-a239cf37d652" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c688ede3-de50-5785-8ead-a239cf37d652" "answer-8c3a6bd9-6993-52af-9d02-30fa0c3f25e1" "answer-b4e835b1-1f34-5eb8-9fd0-8475bfd26768" "answer-091b64ac-c431-5b8f-af2d-93c9caba880c" "answer-7fb2a832-8c31-59ac-a20c-4c5063f88557"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c688ede3-de50-5785-8ead-a239cf37d652" :answer/id #uuid "c688ede3-de50-5785-8ead-a239cf37d652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c3a6bd9-6993-52af-9d02-30fa0c3f25e1" :answer/id #uuid "8c3a6bd9-6993-52af-9d02-30fa0c3f25e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4e835b1-1f34-5eb8-9fd0-8475bfd26768" :answer/id #uuid "b4e835b1-1f34-5eb8-9fd0-8475bfd26768" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-091b64ac-c431-5b8f-af2d-93c9caba880c" :answer/id #uuid "091b64ac-c431-5b8f-af2d-93c9caba880c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fb2a832-8c31-59ac-a20c-4c5063f88557" :answer/id #uuid "7fb2a832-8c31-59ac-a20c-4c5063f88557" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221955 :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe a **stable** equilibrium point.\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium\n{{field-2}} \nthe \n{{field-3}} \nas \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium $\\text{but may not converge to}$ the $\\text{equilibrium}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f131aa45-b457-59cb-b20c-8d7c35137875" "answer-field-98d4933d-22cc-519e-9f30-1dc1238c6246" "answer-field-b7318970-d325-5a77-90ca-e5bc8bfab71c" "answer-field-54c2a1b0-d6e4-52a7-ab45-83f874c7e803"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f131aa45-b457-59cb-b20c-8d7c35137875" :answer-field/id #uuid "f131aa45-b457-59cb-b20c-8d7c35137875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e24d08e2-1fa3-5d55-a408-d06d6f1eec89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1115c611-afc1-502d-b2ee-dd7d365f08c6" "answer-e24d08e2-1fa3-5d55-a408-d06d6f1eec89"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1115c611-afc1-502d-b2ee-dd7d365f08c6" :answer/id #uuid "1115c611-afc1-502d-b2ee-dd7d365f08c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "away from" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e24d08e2-1fa3-5d55-a408-d06d6f1eec89" :answer/id #uuid "e24d08e2-1fa3-5d55-a408-d06d6f1eec89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "close to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98d4933d-22cc-519e-9f30-1dc1238c6246" :answer-field/id #uuid "98d4933d-22cc-519e-9f30-1dc1238c6246" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-940e1f1a-ec4b-543b-b2bf-4fcb2762450a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8d164d7-5d4d-57a0-b394-9818b5ea9254" "answer-940e1f1a-ec4b-543b-b2bf-4fcb2762450a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8d164d7-5d4d-57a0-b394-9818b5ea9254" :answer/id #uuid "b8d164d7-5d4d-57a0-b394-9818b5ea9254" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "and converge to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-940e1f1a-ec4b-543b-b2bf-4fcb2762450a" :answer/id #uuid "940e1f1a-ec4b-543b-b2bf-4fcb2762450a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "but may not converge to" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7318970-d325-5a77-90ca-e5bc8bfab71c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7318970-d325-5a77-90ca-e5bc8bfab71c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-328f9205-a9cf-5f2a-86df-fbc1b8a89ea4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-328f9205-a9cf-5f2a-86df-fbc1b8a89ea4" "answer-00e92b02-6a9b-5d57-8c16-a54ee1f5ab06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-328f9205-a9cf-5f2a-86df-fbc1b8a89ea4" :answer/id #uuid "328f9205-a9cf-5f2a-86df-fbc1b8a89ea4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "equilibrium" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00e92b02-6a9b-5d57-8c16-a54ee1f5ab06" :answer/id #uuid "00e92b02-6a9b-5d57-8c16-a54ee1f5ab06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "infinity" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-54c2a1b0-d6e4-52a7-ab45-83f874c7e803" :answer-field/id #uuid "54c2a1b0-d6e4-52a7-ab45-83f874c7e803" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1747e7bb-f792-5fd2-924f-7a609818f888" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ab41b09-3bf4-5cc7-b502-0e07b72fde80" "answer-1747e7bb-f792-5fd2-924f-7a609818f888"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ab41b09-3bf4-5cc7-b502-0e07b72fde80" :answer/id #uuid "9ab41b09-3bf4-5cc7-b502-0e07b72fde80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\to 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1747e7bb-f792-5fd2-924f-7a609818f888" :answer/id #uuid "1747e7bb-f792-5fd2-924f-7a609818f888" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\to \\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186374742 :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe a **stable** equilibrium point.\n\nThe solutions that start \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-2}}\nit for all future time, and\n{{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186396844 :question/problem "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe an **unstable** equilibrium point.\n\n{{field-1}} solutions that start\n{{field-2}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-3}}\nthe equilibrium." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222490 :tutorial/content "Recall that for $x'(t) = f(x),$ a point $x^\\ast$ is an *equilibrium* if $f(x^\\ast) = 0.$ Solutions starting at $x^\\ast$ remain constant for all $t.$\n\nNow, consider a system in the plane with variables $x(t)$ and $y(t){:}$\n![](images/92/92ee0672fab8119d72b73787d926a36b921e62ab18b5b6c782af901c7a1e491c.svg)\n\nA point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = (x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ is an **equilibrium point** if $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$ Both components must be zero:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0 \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ then $\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ for all $t.$ The state does not change.\n\n**Note:** In the phase plane, $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ represents velocity. At an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ the velocity is $\\mathbf{0},$ so trajectories starting there stay there forever.\n\nFor example, let's find the equilibrium points of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=x-y \\\\ {y}^{′}=x+y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** Set the right-hand sides equal to zero.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=0 \\\\ x+y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Solve the system. From the first equation, $y = x.$ Substitute into the second:\n\n$$\nx + (x) - 2 = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2x = 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, $y = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the system has a single equilibrium point ![](images/59/5939547026b84f5a36fd2f26d7390db4c5069cdf3302217b5309699e5afe8882.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222491 :tutorial/content "So far, we have learned how to find equilibrium points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y).\n$$\n\nHowever, just knowing where equilibrium points are is not enough: we\nalso want to understand *how nearby solutions behave* when we start\nclose to an equilibrium point.\n\nRoughly speaking, there are three qualitatively different behaviors.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **stable** if for\nany neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ there exists a neighborhood\n$O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at\nsome time $t = t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it for all later times,\nalthough they need not converge to it.\n\n![](images/ca/ca05b3e5d076c74f4d3cf346e0598e99d363a155d41ef10b98d1914602638b76.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **asymptotically\nstable** if it is stable *and* there exists a neighborhood $O$ of\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$\napproaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it *and* converge to it as\n$t \\to \\infty.$\n\nNote that asymptotic stability is a stronger condition than stability:\nit adds the requirement that nearby solutions actually reach\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ in the limit.\n\n![](images/ce/cedd8e777a57901042cb46889650006a9b6e1980dc733d736790e3cc3e3ccfaa.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **unstable** if it\nis not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means there is some neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ from which trajectories can escape, even when started\narbitrarily close to $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Note that this does not\nrequire trajectories to stay outside $O$ forever; it only requires them to\nleave $O$ at some moment, even if they return later.\n\n![](images/f7/f7668e3f5ba2b8dc7b4ca3a3e503c55a54a00c2f99648895e052447274bb6d15.png)\n\n**Watch out!** These descriptions are *local*: they describe what\nhappens when the initial condition is sufficiently close to the\nequilibrium. They do not require that all trajectories in the entire\nplane behave the same way.\n\nNext, let's see some examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221469 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
